		{
			"id": "114003979095102972",
			"parentId": "114003883813060811",
			"url": "https://techhub.social/@develwithoutacause/114003979052535710",
			"author": {
				"name": "Doug Parker 🕸️",
				"url": "https://techhub.social/@develwithoutacause",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/109/332/518/937/677/894/original/e1e878ed6a905b12.jpg"
			},
			"date": "2025-02-14T19:51:08.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> <span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://mastodon.social/@jakelazaroff\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>jakelazaroff</span></a></span> Ooh duplicating the module between `?define` and not seems pretty rough, though it seems like that's an issue even outside a bundler?</p><p>Limiting all consumers to either `?define` or not seems pretty constraining at scale.</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "114004059584248977",
			"parentId": "114003893631117216",
			"url": "https://toot.community/@wavebeem/114004059513552270",
			"author": {
				"name": "Sage 🌿",
				"url": "https://toot.community/@wavebeem",
				"avatarUrl": "https://fediverse.zachleat.com/avatars/original/missing.png"
			},
			"date": "2025-02-14T20:11:36.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> i've been a little unsure how this works out in practice though... like what's the use case for defining it with a new name? i guess if you just don't like the name that could be useful, and as long as it's not part of a larger system of components with dependencies on each other...</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "114004299304101337",
			"parentId": "114003979095102972",
			"url": "https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat/114004299304101337",
			"author": {
				"name": "Zach Leatherman",
				"url": "https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/accounts/avatars/109/286/461/031/266/152/original/dc489c3746a44e00.png"
			},
			"date": "2025-02-14T21:12:35.300Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://techhub.social/@develwithoutacause\" class=\"u-url mention\">@<span>develwithoutacause</span></a></span> <span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://mastodon.social/@jakelazaroff\" class=\"u-url mention\">@<span>jakelazaroff</span></a></span> well if you’re wanting both mechanisms you probably wouldn’t do import &quot;./nimble.js&quot; with import &quot;./nimble.js?define&quot;, you’d do import &quot;./nimble.js&quot; with `Nimble.define()`—right?</p><p>(which to me seems like yet another vote for opt-in rather than opt-out)</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "114004307896121308",
			"parentId": "114004059584248977",
			"url": "https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat/114004307896121308",
			"author": {
				"name": "Zach Leatherman",
				"url": "https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/accounts/avatars/109/286/461/031/266/152/original/dc489c3746a44e00.png"
			},
			"date": "2025-02-14T21:14:46.404Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://toot.community/@wavebeem\" class=\"u-url mention\">@<span>wavebeem</span></a></span> for sure — it’s just a preference for small projects. I can see the argument that you might not want renames for larger projects</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "114004332938456842",
			"parentId": "114004299304101337",
			"url": "https://techhub.social/@develwithoutacause/114004332895354049",
			"author": {
				"name": "Doug Parker 🕸️",
				"url": "https://techhub.social/@develwithoutacause",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/109/332/518/937/677/894/original/e1e878ed6a905b12.jpg"
			},
			"date": "2025-02-14T21:21:07.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> <span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://mastodon.social/@jakelazaroff\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>jakelazaroff</span></a></span> Sure, you can not use the feature, but you need to *know* you can't use it in that situation.</p><p>Any given file can import `?define` but it needs to be confident that no *other* file imports without `?define`, which seems hard to enforce.</p><p>I guess you could codify this pattern into your bundler or something and rewrite the import / throw an error, but it's still complexity which needs to be managed.</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "114004357434166162",
			"parentId": "114004332938456842",
